course: adult-life
lesson: 50
title: Rumours about classmates and what to do with a secret
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Knowing about gossip is one thing; knowing what to do when it lands on you is the grown-up part. Sometimes you are the one who hears it, sometimes the one tempted to share it, and sometimes the one it is about.
explain: Good habits help: check a proper news site, look at the date and the account's name, slow down when a story makes you angry fast, and change nothing to make it funnier.
explain: Vivid example: in Wellington, your big brother is about to screenshot his mate's private message and send it to the group. Dad stops him: "Would Tama want that out there? No? Then it stays between you two, bro."
explain: Gossip that hurts is different from telling a trusted grown-up something worrying; if someone may be in danger, telling is the right thing, not tattling. And if people gossip about you, you never have to deal with it alone.
fact: Celebrities are real people with real feelings, and many have said cruel comments hurt them.
fact: Before believing a shocking story, look it up on a proper news site, and check its date.
fact: If a friend is being gossiped about, be kind to her and tell a trusted adult rather than repeating the cruel words.
fact: Accounts that post gossip about real pupils should not be followed or shared; they should be reported.
fact: "Don't believe everything you read, and only half of what you see" is an old saying for being a little doubtful of exciting stories.
fact: Ghost stories at a sleepover are fun because everyone knows they are stories, and nobody gets hurt.
fact: Telling a trusted adult that someone may be in danger is not gossip; it helps.
fact: Before you tell a story about someone, imagine it is about you.
fact: Stories grow as they travel: one mouse in the kitchen becomes "the school's closing because of rats".
fact: Private messages are private; screenshotting and sharing them breaks a friend's trust.
fact: Some stories are written to make you angry so that you share them fast; anger is a sign to slow down.
fact: If you spread something hurtful, say sorry and tell people it wasn't true; you can also simply refuse to listen to cruel gossip.
fact: "Allegedly" means someone has claimed it but it is not proved; a parody account is a joke copying a real name.
fact: Health is the most private story of all, children should be left out of gossip, and judging someone by what they own is unfair.
exercise: Mum: "Celebrities read the comments too." What does she mean? | Famous people have real feelings and can be hurt | Famous people reply to every comment | Comments help famous people get richer and more famous | Famous people never use the internet
exercise: Dad: "Let's look it up on a proper news site before we believe it." Why? | Shocking stories are often untrue until checked | News sites are always more fun to read | It is against the law to believe a post | Proper news sites only ever show good, happy news
exercise: A girl asks, "Shall I tell her what they're saying about her?" What is the best way to help? | Be kind to her and tell a trusted adult | Repeat every mean word so she knows what they said | Join in so they don't pick on you | Pretend you never heard anything
exercise: An account posts gossip about pupils at your school. What should you do? | Report it, and don't follow or share it | Follow it so you know what's going on | Send it your own stories to post | Share it only with your best friends
exercise: Grandad: "Don't believe everything you read, and only half of what you see." What is the advice? | Be a little doubtful of exciting stories | Never read anything ever again | Believe only things on television | Believe everything your friends tell you
exercise: Max says he saw a ghost in the school basement. Is this harmful gossip? | No, it's a story everyone knows is a story | Yes, ghosts have feelings that get hurt by gossip | Yes, the basement will have to close | No, because ghosts are proven real
exercise: The teacher says telling a grown-up something worrying is different from gossip. When should you tell? | When someone may be in danger | Whenever a friend gets a new phone | Only when you are sure to be thanked | Never, because telling is always tattling
exercise: Mum: "What would you want people to say if it were about you?" What is she asking you to do? | Imagine the story is about you before telling it | Make up a nicer story about yourself | Find out what people say about you | Tell the story only to people you like and trust
exercise: "Our school's closing because of rats!" One mouse was seen in the kitchen. What happened to the story? | It grew bigger as it travelled | It was exactly right from the start | The head teacher made it up | It got smaller as it travelled
exercise: Big brother: "I'll just screenshot his message and send it to the group." What's wrong? | A private message is private; it breaks trust | Screenshots make phones run slowly | The group might not find it funny | Nothing, it's his phone so he can share whatever he likes
exercise: Dad: "If a story makes you angry fast, slow down." Why? | It might be written to make you share it | Angry feelings mean the story is true | Slow readers always understand more | Fast anger is good for your health
exercise: You spread something hurtful about a classmate and it wasn't true. What helps most? | Say sorry and tell people it wasn't true | Wait quietly until everyone forgets it happened | Spread a different story to cover it | Blame the person who told you first
exercise: The teacher explains the word "allegedly". What does it mean? | Someone has claimed it, but it isn't proved | A judge has proved it to be true in court | It happened a very long time ago | It is a secret nobody may repeat
exercise: Nan: "I just don't say anything about the royal children." What is her rule? | Leave children out of gossip, famous or not | Only gossip about royal children | Royal children are not real people like us | Only the King can talk about them
exercise: Kids say, "Lucas got a new phone, he must be spoilt." What is fair? | It was a birthday present; don't judge people by what they own | He must be spoilt, nobody else in the class has a phone like that | His parents must have won the lottery | He should give the phone to the class
exercise: Mum: "Rumours about someone's health are never okay." Why? | Health is private; wait for the person to share it | Doctors will be angry if you guess | Health rumours are always true anyway | Illness can be caught by talking about it too much
exercise: Dad: "That 'news' account is a parody. Look at the name." What is a parody account? | A joke account copying a real news name | The official account of a newspaper | An account run by the government | A secret account for news reporters
test: Your mate: "I don't wanna hear it, it's mean." Is she being boring? | No, refusing to listen to cruel gossip stops it | Yes, she'll miss all the good stories | Yes, she should listen to it all first and then decide | No, she just already knows the story
test: Big sister, telling a story: "and then he fell in the pond, well, a puddle, ok I'm making it funnier." What does she know? | Changing a story to make it funnier makes it untrue | Funnier stories are always truer stories | A puddle and a pond are the same thing | You should never tell funny stories at all, to anybody
test: The teacher: "Not everything needs to be shared, some things are just for you." When is keeping it to yourself kindest? | When the news is private for a good reason | Never, sharing is always caring | Only when nobody else will listen | Only when it's boring news anyway
test: A boy shrugs: "But everyone else is talking about it." A classmate's private news is going round. Does that make it okay? | No, it is still private and still hurts | Yes, once everyone knows it's public | Yes, if you only add one small detail | No, unless the teacher is talking too
test: Mum, when you come home upset: "If you hear something about yourself, come and tell me." Why does she say this? | You don't have to deal with it alone | So she can go and shout at the kids | So she can spread it to other mums | Because the rumour must be true then
test: Dad scrolls: "Look at the date — this story's from six years ago, they're sharing it like it's new." Why check dates? | Old news shared as new can mislead and reopen wounds | Old stories are always completely false | New stories are always completely true | Dates on stories are just there for decoration and mean nothing
test: Your cousin: "Telling Miss that big lads keep threatening Kai at the bus stop is snitching, don't." Put him right. | Telling a trusted adult when someone may be in danger is right | He's right, you should keep every secret a friend tells you, whatever it is | Tell Kai's mates instead of a teacher | Post it online so someone helps him
test: Big brother: "wait till u see this, it'll make u SO mad, share it share it." What should you do first? | Slow down and check before sharing | Share it at once while you are mad | Add your own angry words and share | Send it to the person it's about
test: News on telly: "The footballer allegedly kicked a fan." Your little brother: "So he did it!" Put him right. | Allegedly means it's claimed, not proved | Allegedly means the police saw it | Allegedly means he said sorry already | Allegedly means it happened twice
test: Nan, firmly: "You can enjoy a royal wedding on the telly without being cruel about anybody." Which matches her rule? | Enjoy the story, remember there's a person in it | Only watch if you agree with everybody | Never watch anything about famous people | Be as cruel as you like, because they can never hear you
test: Your friend reads a parody account: "the PM says school is cancelled forever!!" What gives it away? | The account's name shows it's a joke | The Prime Minister always posts at night | The post has lots of exclamation marks | School can only be cancelled in summer
exam: Identify the circumstance in which the communication of information concerning another person to a trusted adult does not constitute gossip. | Where that person may be in danger | Where the information is especially amusing | Where the information concerns possessions | Where the adult has requested entertainment
exam: Determine the meaning of the word "allegedly" as employed in the reporting of news. | That a claim has been made but has not been proved | That a court has established the claim as true beyond doubt | That the event in question occurred long ago | That the claim has been withdrawn by its author
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning reports which provoke immediate anger in the reader. | They may be designed to provoke sharing, and warrant caution | They are invariably more accurate than calm reports | They ought to be shared prior to being read in full | They are published exclusively by official sources
exam: Whereas a pupil has disseminated an untrue and hurtful statement concerning a classmate, ascertain the remedy which this lesson commends. | An apology, together with a correction to those informed | Silence, in the expectation that it will be forgotten | The circulation of a further statement to distract | The attribution of responsibility to the original source
